<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balšić noble family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Balšići, House of Balšić</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Balšić were a noble family that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, roughly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, from about 1360 to 1421 after the break-up of the Serbian Empire. From their seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> they fought Ragusa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Albanian lords. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-ii">Balša II</a> was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Savra in 1385, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-iii">Balša III</a> died in 1421 the line ended and Zeta passed to the Serbian Despotate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family.jpg" alt="Balšić noble family" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Illustration by Kj1595 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stema_e_Balshajve.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-i">Balša I</a>, first appears in a charter of the Serbian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> dated 29 September 1360. Writing in 1601, Mavro Orbini described him as a petty nobleman who held a single village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a> under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Dušan the Mighty</a>. After Dušan's death Balša and his three sons took Lower Zeta by acquiring the lands of gospodin Žarko and Upper Zeta by murdering the voivode Đuraš Ilijić, and Uroš the Weak acknowledged them as lords of Zeta.</p><p>Their ethnic origin is disputed. Medieval Serbian documents call them Arbanas lords, Constantine the Philosopher called Đurađ II and Balša III Albanian lords, and Ragusan records mention the Albanian customs of the Balša. The historian Malcolm thought them probably of Albanian stock but largely Serbianized; other scholars hold them Serbian, Slavic or Slavicized Vlachs. Đurađ II himself claimed descent from the Nemanjić family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The three brothers converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1368 or 1369. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> and the collapse of Serbian central authority they ruled as independent lords. According to the historian John V. A. Fine, their lands reached from Peć and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> to the coast and from the Gulf of Kotor south to the Mati River, and through marriage they also gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>. Between 1368 and 1389 they kept their own ships and raided as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> tolerated because of their stand against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p>Their holdings shrank from the end of the 1370s. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrtko-i-of-bosnia">Tvrtko I of Bosnia</a> annexed the coast between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> and other lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, so that under Đurađ II only the land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> and the sea was securely held. The heads of the house were Đurađ I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-ii">Balša II</a>, Đurađ II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-iii">Balša III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-iii">Balša III</a> died in 1421 without an heir, leaving his lands to his uncle, Despot Stefan the Tall. Venice took Bar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a> the same year, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and Bosnians seized other territories. Members of the family founded and renovated several Eastern Orthodox monasteries and churches, and supporters of the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> later claimed descent from them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balšić noble family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Facing threats from the <b>Balšići</b> and the Ottomans, Kotor placed itself under Venice in 1420.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-kotor">Commune of Kotor</a></li><li><blockquote>On 2 March 1444, in the Venetian-held town of Lezhë, Skanderbeg gathered the leading Albanian nobles, among them the Arianiti, Dukagjini, Muzaka, Thopia, Zaharia and <b>Balšić</b> families and Stefan Crnojević of Zeta.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muzaka ruled from the fortress of Berat over Albanian peasants and herders, balancing Byzantium, the Serbs, Venice and the <b>Balšić</b> lords of Zeta.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-berat">Principality of Berat</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1387 they held the port of Lezhë and its hinterland, between the territories of the <b>Balšić</b> lords of Zeta and the Kastrioti.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-dukagjini">Principality of Dukagjini</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Balšići</b> ruled as lords of Zeta with nobles and coastal towns, fighting Ragusa, Bosnia and Albanian lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a></li><li><blockquote>After the decline of the <b>Balšić</b> lords and the Serbian Despotate, the Crnojević family took the lead in the mountains of Zeta.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-crnojevici">Zeta under the Crnojevići</a></li></ul></section>
